parts.append('</table><p>There is no detectable within-group association here between Q1 and accuracy/correctness flags (r=−0.114, p=0.891). This does not establish independence or show that completeness never relates to correctness; the Q1 scale is coarse and the sample is small.</p>')
parts.append('''<h3>Source-type associations within datasheet sections</h3><p>For each source type, record, and section, compare the proportion of populated receipt-linked root fields receiving a review flag between (a) fields linked to that source type and (b) other receipt-linked fields in the same section. Both sets must be nonempty. Average eligible section contrasts equally within record, then records within dataset, then datasets. This accounts for broad section composition, but not field-specific difficulty, source overlap, or differences in claims within a root field.</p><ul><li><strong>Publication-linked fields:</strong> completeness-flag prevalence is 20.3 percentage points higher than the same-section comparison fields on average, positive in all three represented datasets (17 eligible records; exact sign-test p=0.25, BH q=0.60).</li><li><strong>Data-resource-linked fields:</strong> accuracy/correctness-flag prevalence is 8.4 points higher, positive in all three represented datasets (18 records; p=0.25, q=0.60).</li><li><strong>NIH-project-page-linked fields:</strong> consistency-flag prevalence is 11.9 points higher, positive in all four datasets (24 records; p=0.125, q=0.60).</li><li><strong>Section mix changes the picture:</strong> the NIH-page completeness contrast is −12.8 points when all linked fields are compared within record, but +3.5 points when comparisons are restricted to eligible fields within the same section and sections are weighted equally. This changes the eligible comparison set as well as weighting; the shift shows why pooled source associations need qualification.</li></ul><p>An exact two-sided sign test uses one averaged contrast per underlying dataset, rather than treating repeated generated records or individual fields as independent datasets. Types represented in fewer than three datasets are described in the data file but not tested. Four source types × three review outcomes give 12 tests, corrected as a family. With only three or four datasets, the smallest possible two-sided p-values are 0.25 and 0.125, respectively. This deliberately coarse test supports descriptions of direction and heterogeneity, not a claim of statistically established source-type differences. Its generalization assumes independent dataset contrasts with equally likely signs under the null; these four purposively selected projects are not a random sample of all datasets.</p>''')
captions['14_source_associations']=('Source associations after accounting for datasheet section','Positive values mean more reviewer-flagged root fields among fields linked to the named source type, relative to other receipt-linked fields in the same record and section. Dots are dataset means, not individual-field observations. Publication contrasts use 17 records; data resources 18; documentation and NIH pages 24. Source types can overlap and comments can concern a different subfield from the cited passage.')
